    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I see math as a puzzle to solve, not a chore. With my vocational diploma, I’ve learned practical math but not advanced theory. I enjoy its logic but prefer physics and art—math is a tool for understanding the world, not the focus of my passion.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "I don’t get anxious about math itself, but I do worry when it feels disconnected from real life. My parents’ different educational backgrounds (one with a PhD, the other only primary school) made me curious about how math applies practically, which keeps me calm.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Last year, I used AI tools to check my algebra homework. It helped me spot mistakes quickly but sometimes gave overly complex explanations. I preferred simple, step-by-step guidance over deep theory.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "Start by writing the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac). If it’s positive, two real solutions; zero, one solution; negative, complex solutions. Finally, plug into the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(discriminant)] / (2a).",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "First, find the derivative f’(x). Set it equal to zero and solve for x. These x-values are critical points. Plug them back into f(x) to get the y-values. Check if they’re maxima or minima using the second derivative test.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA reduces data dimensions by finding directions (principal components) that capture most variance. You compute the covariance matrix, find its eigenvalues and eigenvectors, then project data onto those vectors. The math is linear algebra—don’t worry! It’s foundational for understanding patterns in data.",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs can offer personalized explanations for math problems, adapting to individual learning paces. They can generate step-by-step solutions for common issues, helping students grasp concepts without getting stuck. This makes math more accessible and less intimidating."
